        "Read 2 Timothy 1:7 and 1 John 4:18. Write a one-page analysis of how fear-based rapture preaching contradicts these verses. Then list five specific fear-based rapture scenarios commonly used (missing children, empty clothes, crashing planes, etc.) and for each one, explain why the biblical description of Christ's visible, audible return (1 Thessalonians 4:16-17, Matthew 24:27, 30-31, Revelation 1:7) makes these scenarios impossible.",
      forgeAWeapon:
        "Create a weapon titled 'Fear Is Not the Gospel' contrasting fear-based rapture urgency with the biblical basis for readiness. Show that 2 Timothy 1:7 promises a spirit of power, love, and a sound mind — not terror. True readiness comes from relationship with Christ, understanding of prophecy, and confidence in His visible return — not from the anxiety of being 'left behind.'",
      jeevesDebrief:
        "Critical psychological intelligence acquired, soldier. Fear-based rapture urgency is one of the most effective and most damaging tactics in the futurist arsenal. It converts more people through anxiety than through exegesis. Your task is not to dismiss people's fears but to replace them with truth. When someone says, 'What if the rapture happens tonight?' the answer is not mockery but Scripture: 'Every eye shall see him' (Revelation 1:7). If every eye sees Him, no one will be left wondering whether it happened. The second coming will not be ambiguous.",
