        "Soldier, today we identify the fallacy of begging the question (petitio principii) as it operates in the dispensational gap theory. Begging the question occurs when an argument's conclusion is assumed in its premise. In the dispensational system, this happens constantly with the gap between Daniel's 69th and 70th weeks.\n\nThe argument runs: 'The events of Daniel 9:27 have not yet been fulfilled. Therefore, the 70th week is still future. Therefore, there must be a gap between the 69th and 70th weeks.' But this argument assumes what it needs to prove. It assumes that Daniel 9:27 describes a future Antichrist rather than Christ — which is the historicist reading. If 'he' in Daniel 9:27 refers to Christ, then the events have been fulfilled (Christ confirmed the covenant, caused sacrifice to cease by His death, and the gospel went to the Gentiles), the 70th week is past, and no gap is needed.\n\nThe futurist assumes a future Antichrist reading of Daniel 9:27 to prove that the 70th week is future, which is used to prove that there is a gap, which is used to prove the seven-year tribulation. Every step assumes what the next step needs to prove.",
